   "source": [
    "# Capstone project - Air passengers, start to finish\n",
    "\n",
    "**What is graded is the process, not the number.** A low MASE from one\n",
    "holdout with no intervals scores worse than an honest analysis that\n",
    "concludes the benchmark wins.\n",
    "\n",
    "Full rules and report requirements: `brief.md`. Grading: `rubric.md`. This\n",
    "notebook is the first deliverable; it should run top to bottom in under ten\n",
    "minutes on Colab."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "ab662132",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import sys\n",
    "\n",
    "if \"google.colab\" in sys.modules:\n",
    "    !git clone -q https://github.com/SDAIAAcademy/international-airpassengers-project.git /content/proj\n",
    "    %cd /content/proj\n",
    "    !pip install -q -e .\n",
    "\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "\n",
    "from coursekit import plotting as P\n",
    "from coursekit import scoring\n",
    "\n",
    "P.use_course_style()\n",
    "\n",
    "SEASON_LENGTH = 12\n",
    "H = 12            # forecast horizon for cross-validation\n",
    "H_REPORT = 24     # forecast horizon for the final report"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fe0c2e82",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# 0. The data\n",
    "\n",
    "Monthly international air passenger numbers, 1949-01 to 1960-12: 144\n",
    "observations, no gaps. The Box & Jenkins classic (1970) — twelve years of\n",
    "growth, and a year that does not repeat the same way twice.\n",
    "\n",
    "Fetched from statsmodels' R-datasets on first run; no download step."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "557a7058",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from statsmodels.datasets import get_rdataset\n",
    "\n",
    "raw = get_rdataset(\"AirPassengers\").data\n",
    "t = raw[\"time\"].astype(float).to_numpy()\n",
    "years = np.floor(t).astype(int)\n",
    "months = np.clip(np.round((t - years) * 12).astype(int) + 1, 1, 12)\n",
    "df = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    \"unique_id\": \"Air passengers (Box & Jenkins)\",\n",
    "    \"ds\": pd.to_datetime(pd.Series(years).astype(str) + \"-\" +\n",
    "                         pd.Series(months).astype(str).str.zfill(2) + \"-01\"),\n",
    "    \"y\": raw[\"value\"].astype(float),\n",
    "}).sort_values(\"ds\").reset_index(drop=True)\n",
    "df.head()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "47ee1344",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# TODO: confirm the calendar. How many months, what range, any gaps?\n",
    "#       (Same three checks you ran on the spine on Day 1.)\n",
    "n_obs = len(df)\n",
    "range_ = (df[\"ds\"].min(), df[\"ds\"].max())\n",
    "n_gaps = (pd.date_range(start=range_[0], end=range_[1], freq=\"MS\").difference(df[\"ds\"])).size\n",
    "print(f\"{n_obs} months, {range_[0].date()} to {range_[1].date()}, {n_gaps} gaps\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "8ed196c6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "P.plot_series(df, title=\"Air passengers, 1949-1960\",\n",
    "              ylabel=\"passengers (thousands)\", xlabel=\"\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "249387c7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**First read.** Before any model, write two sentences that describe this\n",
    "series in plain language - the kind a non-forecaster could repeat. (Trend?\n",
    "Seasonality? What happens to the swings as the level grows? Cycle?) These two\n",
    "sentences belong at the top of your report."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7f897b6e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# 1. Exploration and shape\n",
    "\n",
    "The Day 1 toolkit, in order. Every chart carries its claim: a title that\n",
    "says what it shows."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d4eebf42",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**1.1 Decompose.** STL, additive, period 12. Read all three panels: which\n",
    "component does the work, and what does the remainder look like?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "5063b4fd",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from statsmodels.tsa.seasonal import STL\n",
    "\n",
    "series = df.set_index(\"ds\")[\"y\"]\n",
    "series.index.freq = \"MS\"\n",
    "stl_res = STL(series, period=SEASON_LENGTH, robust=True).fit()\n",
    "\n",
    "dcmp = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    \"ds\": df[\"ds\"],\n",
    "    \"observed\": df[\"y\"],\n",
    "    \"trend\": stl_res.trend.values,\n",
    "    \"seasonal\": stl_res.seasonal.values,\n",
    "    \"resid\": stl_res.resid.values\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "P.decomposition_plot(dcmp, cols=[\"observed\", \"trend\", \"seasonal\", \"resid\"],\n",
    "                     title=\"STL decomposition, air passengers (additive, period=12)\",\n",
    "                     x=\"ds\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0ff8c5eb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**1.2 Trend and seasonal strength.** The two numbers from Day 1, computed\n",
    "the same way (textbook formulas on the STL components). State what they\n",
    "imply for model choice."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "30f92580",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "resid = dcmp[\"resid\"]\n",
    "trend = dcmp[\"trend\"]\n",
    "seasonal = dcmp[\"seasonal\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "var_resid = np.var(resid)\n",
    "trend_strength = max(0, 1 - var_resid / np.var(trend + resid))\n",
    "seasonal_strength = max(0, 1 - var_resid / np.var(seasonal + resid))\n",
    "print(f\"trend strength: {trend_strength:.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"seasonal strength: {seasonal_strength:.2f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d40cedaa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**1.3 Autocorrelation.** The ACF of the raw series. What is at lag 12?\n",
    "At 24? What does that tell you before you fit anything?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "de5b4948",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "P.acf_plot(df[\"y\"], nlags=24, title=\"ACF of air passengers, raw series\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8efe063e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# 2. The floor\n",
    "\n",
    "The seasonal naive is the thing everything else has to beat. Fit it, check\n",
    "what it leaves on the table, give it an honest interval."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "58556d43",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from statsforecast import StatsForecast\n",
    "from statsforecast.models import SeasonalNaive\n",
    "\n",
    "floor_sf = StatsForecast(models=[SeasonalNaive(season_length=SEASON_LENGTH)],\n",
    "                         freq=\"MS\", n_jobs=1)\n",
    "floor_fc = floor_sf.forecast(df=df, h=H_REPORT, level=scoring.LEVELS)\n",
    "floor_fc.head()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "6abf8c8b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "P.fan_chart(df, floor_fc.rename(columns={\n",
    "            \"SeasonalNaive\": \"mean\",\n",
    "            \"SeasonalNaive-lo-20\": \"lo-20\",\n",
    "            \"SeasonalNaive-lo-40\": \"lo-40\",\n",
    "            \"SeasonalNaive-lo-60\": \"lo-60\",\n",
    "            \"SeasonalNaive-lo-80\": \"lo-80\",\n",
    "            \"SeasonalNaive-lo-95\": \"lo-95\",\n",
    "            \"SeasonalNaive-hi-20\": \"hi-20\",\n",
    "            \"SeasonalNaive-hi-40\": \"hi-40\",\n",
    "            \"SeasonalNaive-hi-60\": \"hi-60\",\n",
    "            \"SeasonalNaive-hi-80\": \"hi-80\",\n",
    "            \"SeasonalNaive-hi-95\": \"hi-95\",\n",
    "            \"ds\": \"ds\"}),\n",
    "            title=\"Seasonal naive, 24-month forecast\",\n",
    "            history_tail=36, levels=(80, 95))\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5c89871e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**2.1 What is left on the table.** The floor's in-sample residuals are\n",
    "``y_t - y_{t-12}`` over the series. Ljung-Box at 12 and 24 lags. If it is not\n",
    "white noise, what structure is it, and which kind of model eats that\n",
    "structure?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "02213698",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from statsmodels.stats.diagnostic import acorr_ljungbox\n",
    "\n",
    "resid_floor = (df[\"y\"] - df[\"y\"].shift(SEASON_LENGTH)).dropna()\n",
    "lb_floor = acorr_ljungbox(resid_floor, lags=[12, 24], return_df=True)\n",
    "print(lb_floor)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a6e7ec8b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# 3. Your model\n",
    "\n",
    "ETS, the model this course taught properly. Fit it, read back what the\n",
    "search chose, and say whether that matches the shape you found in section 1."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "bcb343bb",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from statsforecast.models import AutoETS\n",
    "\n",
    "ets_sf = StatsForecast(models=[AutoETS(season_length=SEASON_LENGTH)],\n",
    "                       freq=\"MS\", n_jobs=1)\n",
    "ets_fit = ets_sf.fit(df=df)\n",
    "ets = ets_fit.fitted_[0, 0].model_\n",
    "ets[\"method\"]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "74e8e188",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**3.1 Read the components.** Which (E, T, S) variant did the search pick,\n",
    "and what does each letter say about the series? Compare that to your\n",
    "section-1 read: does the model agree with your eyes, and if not, whose\n",
    "answer do you trust and why?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c499c936",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**3.2 The forecast.** The same 24 months, the same levels, the same\n",
    "chart style - so the two can be compared on the same axes."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "1d385c50",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "ets_fc = ets_sf.forecast(df=df, h=H_REPORT, level=scoring.LEVELS)\n",
    "\n",
    "P.fan_chart(df, ets_fc.rename(columns={\n",
    "            \"AutoETS\": \"mean\",\n",
    "            \"AutoETS-lo-20\": \"lo-20\",\n",
    "            \"AutoETS-lo-40\": \"lo-40\",\n",
    "            \"AutoETS-lo-60\": \"lo-60\",\n",
    "            \"AutoETS-lo-80\": \"lo-80\",\n",
    "            \"AutoETS-lo-95\": \"lo-95\",\n",
    "            \"AutoETS-hi-20\": \"hi-20\",\n",
    "            \"AutoETS-hi-40\": \"hi-40\",\n",
    "            \"AutoETS-hi-60\": \"hi-60\",\n",
    "            \"AutoETS-hi-80\": \"hi-80\",\n",
    "            \"AutoETS-hi-95\": \"hi-95\",\n",
    "            \"ds\": \"ds\"}),\n",
    "            title=\"AutoETS, 24-month forecast\",\n",
    "            history_tail=36, levels=(80, 95))\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ddb54593",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# 4. The harness\n",
    "\n",
    "One holdout is not a ranking. Roll the origin eight times, forecast twelve\n",
    "months each time, score the whole distribution - not just the point."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "51fac2f3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sf_cv = StatsForecast(\n",
    "    models=[SeasonalNaive(season_length=SEASON_LENGTH),\n",
    "            AutoETS(season_length=SEASON_LENGTH)],\n",
    "    freq=\"MS\", n_jobs=1)\n",
    "cv = sf_cv.cross_validation(df=df, h=H, step_size=H, n_windows=8,\n",
    "                            level=scoring.LEVELS)\n",
    "cv.head()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "d7b8fd22",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "MODEL_NAMES = [\"SeasonalNaive\", \"AutoETS\"]\n",
    "rows = []\n",
    "for name in MODEL_NAMES:\n",
    "    row = scoring.score_cv(df,cv, model=name, level=80)
    "    row[\"model\"] = name
    "    rows.append(row)
    "RESULTS = pd.DataFrame(rows)
    "RESULTS"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8bfdc778",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**4.1 Read the table.** For each model: where does it beat the floor,\n",
    "where does it lose, and on which of the three metrics? A model that wins the\n",
    "point forecast and loses the distribution is a real result - report both\n",
    "halves. And what does each model's 80% coverage say about its band?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7bb2e6bb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# 5. The report\n",
    "\n",
    "The notebook is evidence; the report is the claim. Two to three pages, for a\n",
    "manager who will not open the notebook:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **The recommendation.** Which model you would ship, in one sentence, and\n",
    "  the one number that earns it.\n",
    "- **The intervals.** How wide, what they covered, and whether the band is\n",
    "  honest. Coverage is the check a band cannot do alone.\n",
    "- **The residuals.** What your model missed. A Ljung-Box p-value is a fact;\n",
    "  a sentence about what it means is the deliverable.\n",
    "- **One change.** A specific next step - data, driver, frequency, horizon -\n",
    "  and what you expect it to do.\n",
    "\n",
    "Rules: every number in the report comes out of this notebook; no number that\n",
    "could not have come out of the harness counts; no leakage."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "484072fd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Optional, for extra credit.** Source a driver (e.g. monthly mean\n",
    "temperature from a public weather archive, Open-Meteo - free, no key), cache\n",
    "it as a CSV, and regress on it - dynamic regression; the cross-validation\n",
    "discipline still applies to the regression's residuals. Or: run the same job\n",
    "through AutoGluon and compare its leaderboard with your harness."
   ]
  }
